import DocLink from '@/components/DocLink.astro';
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
---
<MotionPage slug="limits" title="Motion: limits" description="The current limits of @udixio/motion, family by family, as the package README states them.">
  <p>
    The package is under construction. Its current limits are listed here, family by family, each with what to do about it. The
    page of a family repeats the limits that concern its code, next to that code. All seven verbs exist: <code>appear</code>,
    <code>disappear</code>, <code>emphasize</code>, <code>reveal</code>, <code>scrub</code>, <code>loop</code> and <code>layout</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Definitions and sequences</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If you need the values of a preset to be final, define your own motion: the presets are not calibrated yet.</li>
    <li>If you rely on a definition or a sequence being checked, check it in development: the validator runs in development only.</li>
    <li>If a cascade of a sequence step runs past <code>--udx-motion-stagger-max</code> (400 ms by default), its later delays are capped there. Give the late elements an <code>at</code> delay, which is not capped.</li>
    <li>If a child belongs to a nested sequence, the outer sequence does not count it as one of its steps: time that child in the nested sequence.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Presence, emphasis and reveal</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If a class of yours sets <code>opacity</code>, <code>translate</code>, <code>scale</code> or <code>rotate</code> on an element after its presence, emphasis or reveal has ended, the class has no effect while those end values stay inline. Set those values with the motion instead, or destroy the binding when the class must win.</li>
    <li>If a loop or a scrub moves an element, its animation overrides the same property while it runs: do not animate that property with a class as well.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Values and counters</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If you need a value's number at more than four decimals, compute it yourself: a value's number is rounded to four decimals.</li>
    <li>If you need the pointer outside the element, a pointer value cannot read it: listen to the pointer yourself.</li>
    <li>If the page scrolls inside an element rather than in the window, a scroll value follows the window's scroll, not that of the element: read the element's scroll yourself.</li>
    <li>If a counter's element holds other text, that text is replaced once the counter moves: put the counter in an element of its own.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Gestures</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If you set <code>translate</code> on an element that a gesture binds, the gesture overwrites it while it is bound: move the element with a wrapper instead. <code>destroy()</code> gives back what the gesture wrote.</li>
    <li>If you set <code>touch-action</code> on an element that a gesture binds, the gesture sets its own value when it binds: <code>none</code> for a free drag, <code>pan-y</code> on the x axis, <code>pan-x</code> on the y axis. Set it on a wrapper instead, or call <code>destroy()</code> to get the value back.</li>
    <li>If you need a keyboard nudge of a drag to glide, use a reorder: the keyboard step of a reorder glides, and the nudge of a drag moves at once.</li>
    <li>If a drag must leave the content box of the parent of its element, it cannot: the box is measured when the pointer grabs the element and at each key nudge. Give the drag a parent that holds its whole path.</li>
    <li>If an element must be reordered, give it a parent: an element with no parent cannot be reordered, and development reports it.</li>
    <li>If a swipe is not prevented, the element stays in the DOM with <code>aria-hidden="true"</code>: remove it yourself when you no longer need it.</li>
    <li>If a gesture's spec is invalid, check it in development: a production build trusts the spec. Its name is always checked.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Text</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If a framework adds or removes the children of a split element, the motion does not follow them. Its text changes are followed, its elements are not: keep the children fixed, and change the text.</li>
    <li>If you split a text by lines and it holds an element other than <code>br</code>, the split falls back to words: use <code>br</code> for the line breaks.</li>
    <li>If your text is in a joining script, such as Arabic, Indic or Thai, a split by characters splits it by words instead, so that no letter loses its form: split it by words to say so.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Scroll and scopes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If the visitor scrolls while <code>scrollTo</code> runs, the scroll stops where it is: start it again when you need it.</li>
    <li>If the browser has no view timeline, a scroll link does not follow the scroll, and the element keeps its stylesheet state: keep that state as the fallback. See <DocLink href="/motion/scrub">Scrub</DocLink>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Layout and shared elements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If a resizing element holds more than 24 elements, or nests them more than three levels deep, its content does not keep its natural size during the move: keep it within those limits.</li>
    <li>If a resizing element holds text directly, that text is stretched during the move: wrap it in an element, which keeps its natural size.</li>
    <li>If a moving element needs another <code>transform-origin</code>, the move is wrong: keep the origin at the centre, where the move scales the element.</li>
    <li>If a move is caused by something outside the layout scope, it is not animated: keep the change inside the scope.</li>
    <li>If an element is bound more than 600 ms after the other holder of its shared identity went away, such as an island that hydrates late, it does not morph: hydrate both within that time.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Tokens and the manifest</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If you need final distances or staggers, calibrate the tokens yourself: they are provisional, with low confidence, and they do not come from M3.</li>
    <li>If you use a distance or a stagger token, suppress its translation or its stagger under reduced motion yourself: the tokens alone do not implement the policy.</li>
    <li>If you build on the manifest or the validator, expect changes: both are experimental. The manifest is published as <DocLink href="/motion-manifest.json">motion-manifest.json</DocLink>.</li>
  </ul>
</MotionPage>
